#300 isn't on main yet: it has been waiting in the land queue since 07:55Z and is still there as my time runs out, so I have no verdict, haven't commented on the issue and haven't closed it.

- **Conflict:** the branch conflicted with main only in `PROPOSALS.md`: two sets of new proposals added in the same place. I kept both, and nothing else needed changing.
- **Commit:** `c3dc1134` is the whole #300 change as one commit on top of the current main. Main had only changed docs (`ROADMAP.md`, `HISTORY.md`, `PROPOSALS.md`) since the last land attempt, so the code is the same as what was queued then. It does what the issue asks:
  - `just gates` runs the gates concurrently up to a cap (`GATES_JOBS`), keeps each gate's output, writes the log in a fixed order and prints each gate's wall time.
  - current-generation runs its fixtures and language legs with the same cap.
  - `scripts/gates_test.go` runs the gates with two different caps and checks the verdict is the same.
  - It also fixes the timing assumptions in the stream-expiry, cert-stream, accounting-sink and DiskStoreTest tests that broke when gates ran side by side.
- **Checks I ran:** a syntax check of the changed shell scripts, and `go test` in `scripts/` (`ok … scripts 19.386s`). I did not run the full `just gates` in this run; the land runs the full set before it merges.
- **Checkpoint:** pushed at 10:38Z. It says the land is queued and what is left: get the verdict (fix and land again if it fails), post the outcome comment, close the issue.

If my time runs out first, the manager still lands it and posts the verdict on the issue, and the next run finishes the issue.
